<description>
Pack scattered colored singleton pixels into a 3x3 snake summary ordered by input column.

Input structure
A grid has a dominant background color and up to nine non-background
singleton pixels. The foreground markers occupy distinct columns.

Rule
Sort the foreground pixels from left to right by their input column. Write
them into a 3x3 background canvas in groups of three, reversing every odd
output row: row 0 left-to-right, row 1 right-to-left, row 2 left-to-right.

Key insight
The output is not a spatial crop; it is the column order of the scattered
markers folded into a boustrophedon 3x3 reading pattern.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_in_order",
  "src": "isolated foreground marker pixels s ordered by input column left-to-right",
  "dst": "3x3 output cells d ordered in snake order: row 0 left-to-right, row 1 right-to-left, row 2 left-to-right",
  "match_cond": "rank(s) == rank(d)",
  "transform": "copy each marker color into its matched output cell and leave unused cells as background",
  "grid_shape": "fixed 3x3 output"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 10x10, background color 0
  markers:
    count: 6-9 singleton (1-pixel) non-background pixels
    columns: each marker in a distinct column; rows need not be distinct
    colors: any non-zero ARC colors (1-9); colors may repeat across markers
  output: fixed 3x3 grid with same background color
  rule: |
    Sort markers left-to-right by column. Write them into a 3x3 grid
    in boustrophedon (snake) order: row 0 L-to-R, row 1 R-to-L, row 2 L-to-R.
    Unfilled cells (when count < 9) remain background; unfilled cells
    appear only at the tail end of the snake order (bottom-right area).

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Choose n_markers uniformly from 6-9.
    2. Build the 3x3 output grid. Pick n_markers non-zero colors (allow repeats).
       Fill the first n_markers cells in snake order; leave the rest as 0.
    3. Read the snake-order color sequence. Sample n_markers distinct columns
       from 0-9 and sort them ascending to establish left-to-right order.
    4. For each marker, pick a random row (0-9). Rows need not be unique.
    5. Place each marker color at (row, col) on a 10x10 all-zero grid.
    6. Input = the 10x10 grid; output = the 3x3 grid from step 2.
```
</generation_spec>
